  truth:`A model for learning. Planet positions are computed from JPL's Keplerian elements for approximate positions (valid 1800–2050) and JPL Small-Body Database elements for the dwarf planets; Mars's 2027 retrograde dates were checked against JPL Horizons. Planet, Sun and Moon facts: NASA Planetary Fact Sheets (updated 18 March 2025), NASA Saturnian Rings Fact Sheet, and NASA science pages (Kuiper Belt, asteroids, Moon formation, Mars moons, Triton, Pluto, dwarf planets, star types); moon sizes and orbits: JPL Solar System Dynamics. Moon counts change as new moons are found, so they're left out. Stars: apparent magnitudes, parallaxes and spectral types from SIMBAD (CDS); absolute magnitude = V + 5 + 5 log₁₀(parallax); temperatures from NASA (Sun), Ohnaka et al. 2013 (Antares) and SIMBAD's catalogued measurements (Sirius: 2018MNRAS.477.3343S; Sirius B: 2011ApJ.743.138G; Canopus: 1989ApJS.70.865R; Alpha Centauri A: 2015A&A.582A.49H; Proxima: 2012ApJ.757.112B; Betelgeuse: 2025A&A.693A.163T; Rigel: 2012A&A.543A.80F; Achernar: 2006MNRAS.371.252L; Arcturus: 2024A&A.684A.85V; Aldebaran: 2023A&A.676A.129H). The main sequence band is from Pecaut and Mamajek (2013). The HR diagram's luminosity scale uses the exam diagram's rule (5 magnitudes = 100 times, Sun +4.83), so it tracks visible light; very hot and very cool stars give out extra light we can't see. Magnitude definitions: IAU Office of Astronomy for Education glossary. Life-cycle thresholds: NASA. The formation timeline, moon replays, life-cycle tracks, belt objects and faint background stars are illustrative; bright background stars are real (SIMBAD, brighter than magnitude 5). Surfaces are generated impressions, not maps, and sizes and distances are not to scale unless the scale control says so. Spin is slowed when time runs fast.`,
